si=si.replace('## S22. Author and release status\n\nThis is an editorial replacement release for author review. The matching GitHub V9.1 commit and public archive DOI were not verified when the files were created. Complete prompts used with AI-assisted systems and any required permissions for pre-existing painted images must be supplied and signed off by the authors prior to journal submission.', '''## S22. AEO2026 reference-case design and comparability boundaries

The official EIA AEO2026 **Counterfactual Baseline Case** is a modelled national energy-system case, not a petroleum-only future. Its vehicle stock includes electric and hybrid vehicles. A complete reference requires 2050 mode-specific travel, 2050 stock and sales by propulsion, fuel economy, replacement schedules, energy use, delivered fuel prices and vehicle ownership costs, all evaluated in consistent dollars. Relevant official source tables are: Table 7 (transport indicators), Table 12 (petroleum product prices), Table 35 (energy by transport mode), Table 36 (fuel by mode), Table 37 (light-duty energy by propulsion), Tables 39–41 (LDV stock, MPG and miles), Tables 43–46 (fleet fuel consumption, sales, stock and distance), Table 49 (freight), and Table 52 (new LDV prices). See EIA, AEO2026, https://www.eia.gov/outlooks/aeo/tables_ref.php.

The current comparison does **not** calculate all of those 2050 fleet ownership and service quantities, and a definitive AEO-matched transport-cost or GHG comparison is therefore **not reported**. The official supplemental tables are identified, but the specific machine-readable 2050 numerical tables were not retrievable during the preparation of this analysis. AEO narrative reports a substantial 2050 electric vehicle stock under policies in force as of December 2025; comparing its 2050 fleet with a petroleum-only 2025 efficiency baseline would conflate scenarios. Fig. S23 and Table S6 are fuel-only sensitivities, not substitutes for EIA data.

## S23. Abatement-cost sensitivity and input provenance

Incremental abatement cost is defined as (petroleum-free annual transport system expenditure minus reference annual expenditure) divided by the reference well-to-wheel emissions less modelled petroleum-free emissions. The historical-price 0%-change reference is US$592.77 bn/y and 2,873 Mt CO₂e/y, and the alternative is US$526.866 bn/y and 189.072 Mt CO₂e/y. The denominator is 2,683.93 Mt for the historical reference. For each gasoline-only reduction, reference direct combustion CO₂ is lowered by (8.9 million bbl/day × 42 gal/bbl × 365 days × 8.887 kg CO₂/gal) times the reduction fraction, while non-gasoline emissions remain fixed. This is a partial WTW screen and may differ from a dynamically recalculated 2050 fleet. Detailed computed values and formulae are archived in `us92_abatement_sensitivity.csv` and `us92_revision.py`.

**Literature and input provenance.** The supplementary source register below restores the wider context cited by model assumptions: methane leakage (Alvarez), resource limits (DOE Billion-Ton and Potential Gas Committee), feedstock and fertilizer data (USDA), reactor-cost anchors (INL and DOE Liftoff), and the limits on Asparagopsis bromoform safety (Eason and later analyses). These sources do not certify assumptions as measured at modeled sites. The use of 1.5% methane leakage remains a screening assumption; Alvarez et al. estimated a higher U.S. supply-chain leakage rate for its study period. Direct land-use and induced land-use emissions for corn ethanol, hemp and bamboo are not fully priced in the central transport result.''')
